Why the AGPL and not a permissive licence.

Overtone is an instrument. Its value is that its measurements can be checked,
and that property survives only if every version anyone runs stays open. The
AGPL's section 13 is the clause that matters here: offering a modified Overtone
to users over a network counts as conveying it, so a hosted fork owes its source
to the people using it. A permissive licence would allow a closed hosted variant
whose numbers nobody could audit, which would defeat the point of the project.

Authorship. All commits prior to 3ad4e5f were authored by Anbuchelvan Ganesan,
committing under the handle Anbu-00001. Sole authorship through Phase 13; the
copyright header carries the legal name from Phase 13 onward. This is recorded
here rather than by rewriting history, so that provenance can be established
from a file in the tree instead of by correlating a handle against commit
metadata -- which matters if the contributor-licence question in CONTRIBUTING
ever becomes live. Sole authorship is the easy case and it is only true now.

